    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "When you are eating those handfuls of food in the dark, do you feel like you are unable to stop or that you don't have control over what or how much you are eating?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "Um, no, I really wouldn't say it's like that. It's not that I can't stop, or that I've lost control of my hands or my mouth or anything dramatic like that. I mean, I know exactly what I'm doing. I'm standing there, usually in my blue slippers—the ones with the hard soles, because the kitchen tile gets so incredibly cold at night—and I'm fully aware that I am eating. \n\nIt's not like a trance or anything. I could absolutely just close the pantry door and walk back down the hall to my bedroom at any second. I just... I choose not to, I guess? It's more like, I'm just standing there, and the house is so incredibly still, you know, where you can hear the refrigerator humming and the clock ticking in the living room, and I just think, well, I'm already here, I might as well have another cracker. And then I have another. I know exactly how many I'm eating, and I know exactly what they are—whether it's the saltines or those little chocolate chip cookies we keep on the middle shelf. \n\nSo, no, I definitely have control. It's not like a runaway train where I'm powerless and just shoving food in my face without thinking. It's not like I wake up the next morning and find a bunch of empty wrappers and wonder what on earth happened. It's just this... this drawn-out process where I keep deciding to have one more, over and over, while I'm just standing there staring into the dark kitchen. I could stop, I just... don't.",
