    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Outside of these eating habits, how have you been feeling in terms of your mood, energy levels, and your interest in doing the things you usually enjoy?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S025",
      "patient_response": "Well, my mood... it's just, I don't know, it's been really hard to pin down, honestly. But the main thing I've noticed, and it's been bothering me for a while now, is that my interest in things is just... gone. It's like the color has been drained out of everything. \n\nI used to have this whole routine, these things that really gave my week structure and joy. Like my book club. We meet every other Tuesday at Sarah's house—she has this gorgeous wrap-around porch, and we always sit out there if the weather is nice, drinking iced tea and discussing whatever historical fiction we chose that month. I used to read the books so carefully, taking notes in the margins, you know, just really looking forward to the discussion. But lately... I haven't even cracked open the last two books. The thought of reading them, of going to the meeting and trying to follow the conversation, it just feels like this enormous chore. \n\nAnd it's not just the book club. It's cooking, too. I used to love trying new recipes, spending a whole Sunday afternoon simmering a sauce or baking bread from scratch, getting flour all over the counter. Now, the idea of chopping an onion feels like climbing a mountain. I just look at the recipe and think, what's the point? It's just so much effort. \n\nEven going to the art galleries downtown, which used to be my absolute favorite thing to do on a Saturday morning... I'd get a coffee, walk through the exhibits, just take it all in. But the last few times I thought about going, I just couldn't muster the energy to get dressed and drive down there. \n\nAnd my rose beds... oh, my roses. I've spent years cultivating them, pruning them just right, making sure the soil pH is perfect. I have these beautiful David Austin varieties, the ones that smell like my grandmother's perfume. Usually, I'm out there every morning with my clippers and my gardening gloves, checking for aphids, deadheading the spent blooms. But now, I just look out the kitchen window at them, and I know they need tending, I know the weeds are creeping in, but I just keep putting it off. I tell myself I'll do it tomorrow, or the next day, but I never do. Everything I used to look forward to just feels so heavy now, like an obligation I just want to avoid.",
