    {
      "turn": 5,
      "doctor_question": "During these periods when you feel that heavy, gray emptiness, have you ever felt or heard things that others couldn't, or held beliefs that felt undeniably true even if others thought they were strange or impossible?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S020",
      "patient_response": "Well, um... it’s funny that you ask that, because I don't know about strange beliefs, really, but there is this... this thing that happens. And it does seem to happen when the house is just so quiet and I have that heavy feeling. It usually happens when I’m completely alone. \n\nLike, my husband will be out, and I'll be in the spare room—I have this little desk in there where I keep all my art supplies from when I used to take classes at the community college. I have all these little plastic bins, you know, the clear ones so you can see exactly what's inside, and I'll be sitting there just sorting through my paints, checking to see which of the watercolor tubes have dried up and which ones are still good. And right while I'm doing that, completely focused on the paints, I will hear my name. Just clear as a bell. Or sometimes it's not my name, it's just a really short remark, like someone saying 'there it is' or 'look.' And it sounds exactly like someone is standing directly behind me, maybe just inches from my shoulder. I'll whip around so fast, fully expecting someone to be standing right there in the room with me, but... there’s never anyone there. The room is completely empty. \n\nAnd, well, it’s not just in the house, either. It happens outside sometimes when I'm watering the roses—those David Austins I was telling you about earlier. I have this heavy, galvanized steel watering can, the vintage kind with the long spout, and I'll be pouring the water, just listening to it soak into the mulch, and I'll hear that same voice, just a quick comment right behind my ear. I'll actually drop the watering can sometimes, water splashing all over my shoes and the patio stones, and I'll spin around thinking a neighbor must have walked up behind me on the grass. But I look, and the yard is just completely empty. There's no one there at all. It's just so vivid, though, I can't help but turn around and look every single time.",
